[x] 65 Fiat Converter -- a second currency converter, for national
       currencies. The FX Converter (PnRate) is named for foreign exchange
       but quotes a crypto market: its 23-entry list is 22 crypto assets and
       USD as the pivot they are priced against, so a worksheet carrying a
       price in euros had no way to read it in forints. The new node is the
       same node with a different quote source, and deliberately the same
       message contract, so a chain converts from one to the other by
       swapping the node.
       [x] 65.1 lib/pn-fiat.[ch], PnFiat, a PnHttp subclass in the core
             (GTK-free) tier, modelled on pn-rate.c property for property:
             from/to/rate/last-update/status, the same mutex discipline
             between the fetch worker and the receive path, the same
             cache-freshness gate on trigger so reopening a worksheet does
             not fire a request, and the same deprecated-rate error marker.
             receive() writes data.value (x rate), data.rate, data.currency
             and data.deprecated -- member for member what PnRate writes.
       [x] 65.2 Quote source: Frankfurter (api.frankfurter.dev/v1/latest),
             a free, key-less, self-hostable front end to the ECB's daily
             reference rates. ?base=<from>&symbols=<to> needs no pivot --
             the endpoint re-bases its euro table on request -- so the parse
             reads one member out of "rates" and is done, where PnRate has
             to divide two USD prices. The reply's "date" is the working day
             the rates were fixed on and goes into the status, because a
             rate fetched on a Sunday is Friday's rate.
       [x] 65.3 Currency set: the 30 the ECB publishes (PnFiatCurrency, its
             own enum, no overlap with PnCurrency beyond USD). Period
             defaults to 3600, which is also PnAutoTrigger's hard ceiling --
             the rates move once a working day. The floor is 600 rather than
             3600 only because a floor equal to the ceiling would make the
             setting a constant the user cannot touch.
